# 🗑️ LeetCode 75 — Day 16: Longest Subarray of 1's After Deleting One Element

**📌 Problem:** [1493. Longest Subarray of 1's After Deleting One Element](https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-subarray-of-1s-after-deleting-one-element/)
**🏷️ Topics:** Array, Sliding Window
**⚡ Difficulty:** Medium

---

## 🧠 Intuition

Deleting one element is only ever worth doing if that element is a `0` — deleting a `1` would just shrink the subarray for no benefit. So the problem really becomes: find the longest window that contains **at most one zero**, and the answer is that window's length *minus* the zero itself (since it gets deleted). Instead of tracking a `zeros` counter and shrinking with a `while` loop, this solution uses a clever shortcut: remember the index of the **last zero seen**, and whenever a new zero shows up, jump the window's start to right after the *previous* zero.

---

## 🛠️ Approach

1. Use `i` (window start), `j` (window end), and `last_zero_idx` (initialized to `-1`) to remember where the most recent zero was.
2. Loop `j` from `0` to the end of `nums`:
   - If `nums[j] == 0`, this is a new zero. Move the window's start to `i = last_zero_idx + 1` — everything before this point (up to and including the *previous* zero) is dropped, since keeping more than one zero in the window isn't allowed. Then update `last_zero_idx = j`.
   - Update `result = max(result, j - i)`. Note it's `j - i`, not `j - i + 1` — this deliberately excludes one element (the current zero, if present) to account for the single deletion.
3. Return `result` after scanning the full array.

**Why `j - i` and not `j - i + 1`:** The window `[i, j]` may contain exactly one zero (at position `j` or somewhere earlier, tracked via `last_zero_idx`). Since that one zero is the element we're "deleting," the actual subarray of 1's we get to keep is one shorter than the raw window size — which `j - i` captures directly.

---

## ⏱️ Complexity

- **Time complexity:** `O(n)` — a single pass through the array; `j` moves forward once, and `i` only ever jumps forward, never backward.
- **Space complexity:** `O(1)` — only a few integer variables are used.

---

## 💻 Code

**Language/Format:** C++ — solved using LeetCode's OOP submission format (`class Solution` with a public method), using a sliding window that tracks the last zero's index instead of a running zero count.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int longestSubarray(vector<int>& nums) {
        int i = 0, j = 0;
        int last_zero_idx = -1;
        int result = 0;

        while (j < nums.size()) {
            if (nums[j] == 0) {
                i = last_zero_idx + 1;
                last_zero_idx = j;
            }
            result = max(result, j - i);
            j++;
        }
        return result;
    }
};
```

---

## 🔑 Key Takeaway

Some sliding-window problems don't need an explicit `while` loop to shrink the window — if the "at most k invalid elements" rule has `k = 1`, tracking just the *position* of the last invalid element is often enough to jump the window's start directly to the right spot, skipping the shrink loop entirely.

